Appendix A. Further reading

Chapter 1. Installing Python

Chapter 2. Your First Python Program

* 2.3. Documenting Functions

* PEP 257 defines @{code}doc string@{body} conventions.

* @{i}Python Style Guide@{ui} discusses how to write a good @{code}doc string@{body}.

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses conventions for spacing in @{code}doc string@{body} s.

* 2.4.2. What's an Object?

* @{i}Python Reference Manual@{ui} explains exactly what it means to say that everything in Python is an object, because some people are pedantic and like to discuss this sort of thing at great length.

* eff-bot summarizes Python objects.

* 2.5. Indenting Code

* @{i}Python Reference Manual@{ui} discusses cross-platform indentation issues and shows various indentation errors.

* @{i}Python Style Guide@{ui} discusses good indentation style.

* 2.6. Testing Modules

* @{i}Python Reference Manual@{ui} discusses the low-level details of importing modules.

Chapter 3. Native Datatypes

* 3.1.3. Deleting Items From Dictionaries

* @{i}How to Think Like a Computer Scientist@{ui} teaches about dictionaries and shows how to use dictionaries to model sparse matrices.

* Python Knowledge Base has a lot of example code using dictionaries.

* Python Cookbook discusses how to sort the values of a dictionary by key.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes all the dictionary methods.

* 3.2.5. Using List Operators

* @{i}How to Think Like a Computer Scientist@{ui} teaches about lists and makes an important point about passing lists as function arguments.

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} shows how to use lists as stacks and queues.

* Python Knowledge Base answers common questions about lists and has a lot of example code using lists.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes all the list methods.

* 3.3. Introducing Tuples

* @{i}How to Think Like a Computer Scientist@{ui} teaches about tuples and shows how to concatenate tuples.

* Python Knowledge Base shows how to sort a tuple.

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} shows how to define a tuple with one element.

* 3.4.2. Assigning Multiple Values at Once

* @{i}Python Reference Manual@{ui} shows examples of when you can skip the line continuation character and when you need to use it.

* @{i}How to Think Like a Computer Scientist@{ui} shows how to use multi-variable assignment to swap the values of two variables.

* 3.5. Formatting Strings

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes all the string formatting format characters.

* @{i}Effective AWK Programming@{ui} discusses all the format characters and advanced string formatting techniques like specifying width, precision, and zero-padding.

* 3.6. Mapping Lists

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses another way to map lists using the built-in @{code}map@{body} function.

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} shows how to do nested list comprehensions.

* 3.7. Joining Lists and Splitting Strings

* Python Knowledge Base answers common questions about strings and has a lot of example code using strings.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes all the string methods.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the @{code}string@{body} module.

* @{i}The Whole Python FAQ@{ui} explains why @{code}join@{body} is a string method instead of a list method.

Chapter 4. The Power Of Introspection

* 4.2. Using Optional and Named Arguments

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses exactly when and how default arguments are evaluated, which matters when the default value is a list or an expression with side effects.

* 4.3.3. Built-In Functions

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents all the built-in functions and all the built-in exceptions.

* 4.5. Filtering Lists

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses another way to filter lists using the built-in @{code}filter@{body} function.

* 4.6.1. Using the and-or Trick

* Python Cookbook discusses alternatives to the @{code}and-or@{body} trick.

* 4.7.1. Real-World lambda Functions

* Python Knowledge Base discusses using @{code}lambda@{body} to call functions indirectly.

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} shows how to access outside variables from inside a @{code}lambda@{body} function. (PEP 227 explains how this will change in future versions of Python.)

* @{i}The Whole Python FAQ@{ui} has examples of obfuscated one-liners using @{code}lambda@{body}.

Chapter 5. Objects and Object-Orientation

* 5.2. Importing Modules Using from module import

* eff-bot has more to say on @{code}import @{i}module@{ui}@{body} @{i}vs.@{ui} @{code}from @{i}module@{ui} import@{body}.

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses advanced import techniques, including @{code}from @{i}module@{ui} import *@{body}.

* 5.3.2. Knowing When to Use self and __init__

* @{i}Learning to Program@{ui} has a gentler introduction to classes.

* @{i}How to Think Like a Computer Scientist@{ui} shows how to use classes to model compound datatypes.

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} has an in-depth look at classes, namespaces, and inheritance.

* Python Knowledge Base answers common questions about classes.

* 5.4.1. Garbage Collection

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes built-in attributes like @{code}__class__@{body}.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the @{code}gc@{body} module, which gives you low-level control over Python's garbage collection.

* 5.5. Exploring UserDict: A Wrapper Class

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the @{code}UserDict@{body} module and the @{code}copy@{body} module.

* 5.7. Advanced Special Class Methods

* @{i}Python Reference Manual@{ui} documents all the special class methods.

* 5.9. Private Functions

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses the inner workings of private variables.

Chapter 6. Exceptions and File Handling

* 6.1.1. Using Exceptions For Other Purposes

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses defining and raising your own exceptions, and handling multiple exceptions at once.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes all the built-in exceptions.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the getpass module.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the @{code}traceback@{body} module, which provides low-level access to exception attributes after an exception is raised.

* @{i}Python Reference Manual@{ui} discusses the inner workings of the @{code}try...except@{body} block.

* 6.2.4. Writing to Files

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses reading and writing files, including how to read a file one line at a time into a list.

* eff-bot discusses efficiency and performance of various ways of reading a file.

* Python Knowledge Base answers common questions about files.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes all the file object methods.

* 6.4. Using sys.modules

* @{i}Python Tutorial@{ui} discusses exactly when and how default arguments are evaluated.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the @{code}sys@{body} module.

* 6.5. Working with Directories

* Python Knowledge Base answers questions about the @{code}os@{body} module.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} documents the @{code}os@{body} module and the @{code}os.path@{body} module.

Chapter 7. Regular Expressions

* 7.6. Case study: Parsing Phone Numbers

* Regular Expression HOWTO teaches about regular expressions and how to use them in Python.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes the @{code}re@{body} module.

Chapter 8. HTML Processing

* 8.4. Introducing BaseHTMLProcessor.py

* W3C discusses character and entity references.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} confirms your suspicions that the @{code}htmlentitydefs@{body} module is exactly what it sounds like.

* 8.9. Putting it all together

* You thought I was kidding about the server-side scripting idea. So did I, until I found this web-based dialectizer. Unfortunately, source code does not appear to be available.

Chapter 9. XML Processing

* 9.4. Unicode

* Unicode.org is the home page of the unicode standard, including a brief technical introduction.

* Unicode Tutorial has some more examples of how to use Python's unicode functions, including how to force Python to coerce unicode into ASCII even when it doesn't really want to.

* PEP 263 goes into more detail about how and when to define a character encoding in your @{code}.py@{body} files.

Chapter 10. Scripts and Streams

Chapter 11. HTTP Web Services

* 11.1. Diving in

* Paul Prescod believes that pure HTTP web services are the future of the Internet.

Chapter 12. SOAP Web Services

* 12.1. Diving In

* http://www.xmethods.net/ is a repository of public access SOAP web services.

* The SOAP specification is surprisingly readable, if you like that sort of thing.

* 12.8. Troubleshooting SOAP Web Services

* New developments for SOAPpy steps through trying to connect to another SOAP service that doesn't quite work as advertised.

Chapter 13. Unit Testing

* 13.1. Introduction to Roman numerals

* This site has more on Roman numerals, including a fascinating history of how Romans and other civilizations really used them (short answer: haphazardly and inconsistently).

* 13.3. Introducing romantest.py

* The PyUnit home page has an in-depth discussion of using the @{code}unittest@{body} framework, including advanced features not covered in this chapter.

* The PyUnit FAQ explains why test cases are stored separately from the code they test.

* @{i}Python Library Reference@{ui} summarizes the @{code}unittest@{body} module.

* ExtremeProgramming.org discusses why you should write unit tests.

* The Portland Pattern Repository has an ongoing discussion of unit tests, including a standard definition, why you should code unit tests first, and several in-depth case studies.

Chapter 14. Test-First Programming

Chapter 15. Refactoring

* 15.5. Summary

* XProgramming.com has links to download unit testing frameworks for many different languages.

Chapter 16. Functional Programming

Chapter 17. Dynamic functions

* 17.7. plural.py, stage 6

* PEP 255 defines generators.

* Python Cookbook has many more examples of generators.

Chapter 18. Performance Tuning

* 18.1. Diving in

* Soundexing and Genealogy gives a chronology of the evolution of the Soundex and its regional variations.

